+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`Flask.do_teardown_request` and `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext` invoke callback
+lists followed by Blinker signals directly. A raised exception interrupts that
+sequence. `AppContext.pop` resets its context variable in a `finally` block,
+but an application teardown failure can currently prevent later signal work.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize collection and deferred raising of teardown failures.
+- Preserve callback ordering and continue across both callbacks and signal
+  receivers.
+- Ensure the context variable reset remains unconditional before deferred
+  errors escape.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Change normal request dispatch or non-teardown signal error behavior.
+- Change teardown callback registration order or exception arguments.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Introduce a small shared Flask helper that runs an iterable of cleanup
+  callables, collects `BaseException` instances, and raises after the iterable
+  is exhausted. This avoids duplicating collection rules between request and
+  application-context teardown.
+- Invoke teardown signal receivers through the same resilient mechanism rather
+  than relying on the signal's aggregate send method, because `send` stops at
+  its first receiver error. Preserve signal sender, arguments, and Flask's
+  async wrapper when invoking each receiver.
+- Use `BaseExceptionGroup` when it exists so every failure is retained. Keep a
+  compatibility fallback that raises the first error on older supported Python
+  versions.
+
+Alternatives considered: nesting `try`/`except` around each call in both
+teardown methods duplicates error policy and does not make signal receivers
+resilient; suppressing failures loses teardown diagnostics.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Signal receiver ordering is managed by Blinker] → Preserve the order
+  returned by the signal's receiver iterator and cover all receivers in tests.
+- [Teardown can now expose grouped errors] → This is the settled compatibility
+  behavior and occurs only after all cleanup completes.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+No deployment or data migration is required. Existing applications retain
+callback ordering; applications that inspect teardown failures may receive an
+exception group on supported Python versions.
