@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+An exception from a teardown callback currently prevents later callbacks and
+the teardown signal from running, which can leave resources unclosed. Teardown
+must make a best effort to finish registered cleanup while still reporting its
+failures.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Run every request and application-context teardown callback even if one or
+  more callbacks fail.
+- Send the corresponding teardown signal after callback failures as part of the
+  same best-effort cleanup.
+- Raise collected teardown failures only after cleanup and context restoration;
+  use an exception group where the running Python supports it.
+- Add regression tests for callback, signal, error aggregation, and
+  context-restoration behavior.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `resilient-teardown-cleanup`: Best-effort request and application-context
+  teardown with deferred error reporting.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+None.
+
+## Impact
+
+Changes `src/flask/app.py` and `src/flask/ctx.py`, with focused coverage in
+the context teardown tests. Applications may observe grouped teardown errors
+on supported Python versions after all cleanup has run.
